WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:06.715
Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.715 --> 00:00:07.840
My name is Michael Chirico.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.580
I'm currently a Data
Scientist at Google,

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:11.247
and I've been
contributing to data.table

00:00:11.247 --> 00:00:12.727
in my free time
for several years.

00:00:12.727 --> 00:00:14.935
I'm here to talk to you
about how to add translations

00:00:14.935 --> 00:00:17.290
to your R packages.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:19.090
So why would you want
to have translations

00:00:19.090 --> 00:00:20.428
for your R packages?

00:00:20.428 --> 00:00:21.970
We're very blessed
in the R community

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:23.800
to have a very global community.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:27.280
Just from a snapshot that we
see in the R-Ladies directory

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:29.290
and in the R RStudio
Community Survey,

00:00:29.290 --> 00:00:31.870
we see that R community
is a very global one

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:35.720
in all the continents with
usage from all over the world.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.140
So by the time that you
release your package,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.910
it will start to
be used by people

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.370
that their native
language is not English.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.620
And a lot of package
developers themselves,

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.280
their native language
is not English.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.940
And kind of motivated by this
and over the years seeing error

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.280
messages reported on the
data.table translation tracker

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.130
from people whose R session
was clearly not in English,

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:01.630
we took up the challenge of
trying to add translations

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:03.310
to data.table.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.980
We landed on doing Chinese,
and we got together

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.980
a team of over 20
translators to work on this.

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:11.230
It ended up being kind of
a monumental undertaking

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:13.270
because data.table is
a pretty old package.

00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.790
Coming with that, there's
over 1,400 error messages

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:17.500
in data.table itself.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.820
I think a typical package
would have less than 100.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.450
A lot of packages have
less than 20 even.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.640
So 1,400 actually took
quite a lot of time

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:31.832
to figure out even how
to go about translating.

00:01:31.832 --> 00:01:33.790
And I'd love to have time
to thank individually

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:34.810
all those people.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.510
It's really an honor to
have been part of it.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.810
But just while we have
time in the short time

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.870
to thank Hongyuan Jia, Zhi Yang,
and Guangchang Yu, who really

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.370
helped get the team together
and get it over the finish line

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:47.150
to get it done.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.858
But the whole time I
was working on this,

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:53.020
I was kind of taken aback
by how much friction

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.560
there is in the process.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.590
There's a lot of
pain points in having

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.110
to learn a whole
new language, which

00:01:58.110 --> 00:02:01.390
is this language of gettext
and pot files, po files,

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.080
and mo files-- all these
things, which you don't really

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:08.032
need to understand that
much to add translations

00:02:08.032 --> 00:02:08.699
to your package.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.449
So over the last
couple of months

00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:13.030
I've been working on a
package called potools.

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.460
The goal is to really eliminate
as much of those frictions

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.502
as possible to make it
easier to add translations

00:02:17.502 --> 00:02:18.370
to the packages.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.890
And as of right now, there's
only one user facing function.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:22.690
It's called translate package.

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:26.380
And it does all the legwork
for you of adding together

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:28.783
all those gettext
facing utilities

00:02:28.783 --> 00:02:30.700
and just walks you through
adding translations

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:32.867
to all the messages that
it finds within your package.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.510
So with that in
mind, we really need

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.440
to think about how to
set your package up

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:40.940
to be translated
in the first place.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.788
There are some general
rules about how

00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:45.580
to do development on
your project that will

00:02:45.580 --> 00:02:47.650
make it easier for translation.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.020
The first one is
about templating.

00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:54.940
Here, the stop message that we
see, it's something very common

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.610
to see and it's used all the
time and in my own development,

00:02:57.610 --> 00:03:00.710
but in terms of translation, it
makes things a bit harder.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.010
What a translator would
see from the first instance

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:04.480
is three strings--

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:07.690
found, columns comma
but, and are needed.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:11.290
And out of context, those things
are very hard to translate.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:14.710
And there are some issues with
duplication that are even harder

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:15.640
to translate.

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.640
If it's in the templated
form, the translator

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.915
is free to rearrange
things, which

00:03:20.915 --> 00:03:23.290
will have to be done in, for
example, Japanese, which has

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:25.810
a totally different grammar.

00:03:25.810 --> 00:03:27.610
And just the
templated form makes

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:31.300
it a lot easier for
translators to really make

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.700
the message appear
as it naturally

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:35.650
would in their own language.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:37.750
It's a similar thing
for pluralization.

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.180
English is blessed to have a
pretty simple pluralization

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.530
system.

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:45.220
When n equals 1 in this
message, the error would become

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.450
"found an issue."

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.780
When n is different from
1, the error should become

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:52.060
"found some issues."

00:03:52.060 --> 00:03:53.708
And, for example,
East Asian languages

00:03:53.708 --> 00:03:55.750
is typically even easier,
that there's not really

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:57.580
any pluralization
in the first place.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.580
But for some languages
like Slovenian and Arabic,

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.720
there are up to four or even
six types of pluralization

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.750
that have to be
handled by translators.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.900
And for that, there is
this ngettext function

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:13.990
that is part of base
R that you provide

00:04:13.990 --> 00:04:16.258
it the number and some
template translations,

00:04:16.258 --> 00:04:17.800
and then you're
translator would help

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.649
do the legwork of providing
what the translation should

00:04:20.649 --> 00:04:23.620
be based on what n is.

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:26.440
And that's all I have time for
today in the lightning talk.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:28.630
So thanks to everybody.

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.310
Thanks to RStudio
for the invite.

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.160
And thanks to all the
contributors to the languages

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.050
and thank you.

00:04:38.050 --> 00:04:40.500
[GUITAR PLAYING]

